    [7, "Chandra Bhedana (left-nostril / lunar)", "Gheranda Samhitā; later Hatha manuals", "Public", "Inhale left 4–6 s, retain 8–12 s, exhale right 8–12 s. 5–10 rounds.", "3–4", "Seated", "Cooling, calming; lunar current.", "Left-nostril breathing associated with parasympathetic shift and lower BP in small trials.", "Weak.", "Evening before the track.", "—"],
    [8, "Śītalī (curled-tongue cooling breath)", "Hatha (HYP 2.57–58)", "Public", "Curl the tongue into a tube, inhale through it 4–6 s (cool), close mouth, exhale through the nose 6–8 s. 10–20 rounds.", "5–6", "Any", "Cools, cures fever, hunger and thirst; 'the yogi becomes like a serpent'.", "Evaporative cooling of the tongue and inspired air; slow rate; mild parasympathetic effect.", "Weak.", "Return, or any time you overheat under the blanket.", "—"],
    [9, "Śītkārī (hissing breath)", "Hatha (HYP 2.54–56)", "Public", "Teeth together, lips apart: inhale with a hiss 4–6 s, exhale through the nose 6–8 s.", "5–6", "Any", "Cooling; 'becomes beautiful as the god of love'.", "As Śītalī without the tongue curl.", "Weak.", "Return.", "—"],
    [10, "Mūrcchā (fainting breath)", "Hatha (HYP 2.69)", "Public (but rarely taught)", "Inhale fully, hold with firm chin lock until the edge of fainting, exhale slowly.", "—", "Seated", "'Makes the mind swoon' — bliss through near-unconsciousness.", "Prolonged apnoea with Valsalva: CO2 rise, O2 fall, reduced venous return, pre-syncope.", "None.", "Not used. Listed for completeness.", "Syncope risk; never lying alone, never in water."],
    [11, "Plāvinī (floating breath)", "Hatha (HYP 2.70)", "Public", "Swallow air into the stomach until the belly is full, hold, release.", "—", "Any", "Lets the yogi float on water 'like a lotus leaf'.", "Gastric air insufflation; nothing more.", "None.", "Not used.", "Bloating."],
    [12, "Kevala Kumbhaka (spontaneous breath suspension)", "Hatha (HYP 2.71–74); Śiva Saṃhitā; the goal of all sahita (assisted) kumbhakas", "Public as a concept; arises on its own",
     "Not performed. When the breath stops by itself in deep absorption, neither inhaling nor exhaling, with no effort — that is kevala ('alone'). The texts say practising the eight kumbhakas leads to it.", "0 for 10–60 s", "Any",
     "'When kevala kumbhaka is attained, nothing in the three worlds is unattainable'; the state of rāja-yoga; mind and prāṇa dissolve together (laya).",
     "At sleep onset and in deep absorption, metabolic rate and CO2 drive fall and breathing becomes periodic with long pauses (central apnoeas of 10–40 s are normal at this edge). Attention stabilises when respiratory motion stops — fewer interoceptive 'events'.",
     "Traditional; the physiology of breath pauses at theta depth is well described.", "Drum Gate plateau and No-Time: welcome it, never force it.", "Never force a pause; it must come alone."],
    [13, "So'haṃ / Haṃsa ajapa-japa (the unrepeated mantra of the breath)", "Haṃsa, Dhyānabindu and Yogacūḍāmaṇi Upaniṣads; Bihar School 'ajapa japa' series; Kashmir Shaivism (Vijñāna Bhairava v. 155)", "Public",
     "'So' on the inhale, 'Haṃ' on the exhale (or Haṃ-sa, the reverse). The texts say the breath does this 21,600 times a day by itself; the practice is only to notice. Bihar version adds Ujjāyī, khecarī and a visualised current up and down the spine.", "natural (15) down to 4–6", "Any",
     "'I am That' (sa aham): the breath itself is the mantra of the Self; leads to the anāhata nāda and to the void (śūnya) at the turning points.",
     "Pairing a syllable with each phase of breath is a sustained-attention anchor; it slows breathing when done deliberately. 21,600/day = 15 breaths/min, the scriptural 'natural rate' — close to measured adult rest.", "Traditional; attention effects moderate.", "Descent (with Ujjāyī 1:2).", "—"],
    [14, "Nāda Anusandhāna with Ṣaṇmukhī / Yoni mudrā (listening to the inner sound)", "Hatha (HYP 4.65–102); Nāda Yoga; Kriya lineages' 'Aum technique' (Yogananda) is the same practice with the arms on a rest", "Public; Yogananda's version taught in his Lessons",
     "Thumbs close the ears, index fingers rest on closed eyes, other fingers on the nose/lips. Breathe slowly. Listen in the right ear for inner sounds (ringing, bell, conch, flute, thunder) and follow the subtlest one. 5–20 min.", "4–6", "Seated or lying",
     "The mind is 'snared' by the inner sound and dissolves (laya); ten sounds are heard in sequence; the last is thunder; samādhi follows.",
     "Closing the ears raises the audibility of otoacoustic emissions, blood flow and the spontaneous 'ringing' most people can hear in silence (tinnitus-like); attention to them is a strong absorption device. Our track places bowl/bell cues slightly right in homage.",
     "Traditional; absorption physiology moderate.", "Ajna and No-Time if you prefer listening over toning.", "Light pressure on the eyes only."],
    [15, "Khecarī mudrā (tongue to the nasopharynx)", "Hatha (HYP 3.32–54); Kriya lineages (talabya kriya prepares it)", "Public description; the full form traditionally given by a guru",
     "Tongue rolled back to touch the soft palate / uvula during slow breathing and retention. Classical texts describe lengthening the tongue over years (frenulum cutting is NOT recommended).", "—", "Any",
     "Stops the 'fall of nectar' (amṛta) from the sahasrāra, conquers hunger, thirst, sleep and death; the seal of all seals.", "Palatal stimulation and swallowing reflex suppression; mild increase in salivation ('nectar'); in retention it helps seal the airway. No measured endocrine effect.", "Traditional.", "Optional during kriya pranayama (Descent).", "Never cut the frenulum."],
    # ---- Kriya Yoga
    [16, "Hong-Sau technique", "Kriya Yoga (Paramahansa Yogananda, SRF Lessons; also Ananda)", "Public (taught in the printed Lessons)",
     "Watch the breath without controlling it. Mentally 'Hong' on the inhale, 'Sau' on the exhale; gaze gently at the point between the eyebrows; as the breath calms, enjoy the pauses.", "natural → very slow", "Seated (lying acceptable here)",
     "Calms the breath and heart so that life force withdraws from the senses into the spine; prepares for Kriya proper.", "Identical in mechanism to So'ham: an attentional anchor that slows respiration; the upward gaze is Shambhavi.", "Traditional; attention physiology moderate.", "Threshold / early Descent.", "—"],
    [17, "Kriya Pranayama (spinal breathing)", "Kriya Yoga: Lahiri Mahasaya (1828–95) → Sri Yukteswar → Yogananda; SRF, Ananda, Kriya Yoga International, Yogiraj (Satyeswarananda) lineages", "Initiatory in every lineage (SRF after ~1 year of Lessons; vows of secrecy); public reconstructions exist (Ennio Nimis 'Kriya Yoga: synthesis of a personal experience', 2009; Lahiri's own notes published by Yogiraj Publications)",
     "Seated: with a soft throat (Ujjāyī-like) sound, inhale 10–15 s feeling a cool current rise along the spine from mūlādhāra to the point between the eyebrows (some lineages: to the medulla/bindu); exhale 10–15 s feeling a warm current descend. 12 breaths = one unit; 12 to 24 units a sitting for beginners, hundreds for advanced practitioners. Followed by Mahā mudrā, Navi kriya, Yoni (Jyoti) mudrā.", "2–3", "Seated (lying works for the breathing itself)",
     "Magnetises the spine, draws life force from the senses, 'one Kriya = one solar year of natural evolution' (Autobiography of a Yogi ch. 26: 1,000 Kriyas in eight hours = 1,000 years); leads to the spiritual eye (the star in the circle of light at the brow) and cosmic consciousness.",
     "Very slow breathing (2–3/min) with a 1:1 ratio: maximal RSA, mild CO2 accumulation (warmth, heaviness, 'current'), strong interoceptive sweep along the midline. The 'cool up / warm down' sensation is temperature perception of inspired vs expired air projected onto the spine by attention. Evolution-per-breath claim is lineage doctrine.",
     "Traditional; slow-breathing physiology strong.", "Descent 9:00–11:00 (quiet window).", "Dizziness from over-slow breathing: shorten the counts."],
    [18, "Jyoti (Yoni) mudrā — seeing the spiritual eye", "Kriya Yoga (Lahiri's 'Yoni mudrā'; Yogananda's 'Jyoti mudrā')", "Initiatory (public descriptions exist)",
     "After kriya pranayama: inhale, close ears with thumbs and eyes with fingers, hold the breath comfortably, gaze into the darkness at the brow for the inner light (golden ring, blue field, white star). Release, breathe, repeat 3–5 times.", "—", "Seated",
     "Reveals the spiritual eye (kūṭastha): a gold ring, a blue field, a white five-pointed star — the door to cosmic consciousness.", "Phosphenes and the 'eigengrau' of the dark-adapted visual system, modulated by retention-induced changes in retinal blood flow and by eye pressure; the concentric colour pattern is the classic entoptic description.", "Traditional; entoptic physiology well known.", "Ajna stage (replaces toning if you prefer).", "Short holds only."],
    [19, "Aum technique (Yogananda)", "Kriya Yoga / SRF", "Public (Lessons)", "Arms on a rest, Shanmukhi-like closure of the ears, listen for the cosmic sound Aum behind the inner sounds, breath slow and forgotten.", "slow", "Seated", "Hearing the Aum vibration = communion with the creative vibration of the cosmos.", "As nāda anusandhāna (#14).", "Traditional.", "No-Time (listening posture).", "—"],
    # ---- Kundalini Yoga (3HO / Yogi Bhajan)
    [20, "Breath of Fire", "Kundalini Yoga as taught by Yogi Bhajan (3HO, from 1969; claimed a secret 'Raj Yoga' lineage — unverified)", "Public",
     "Rapid equal inhale/exhale through the nose from the navel, 2–3 breaths/s, 1–11 min, usually with a posture (kriya).", "120–180", "Seated/posture", "Heats, oxygenates, 'charges the aura', releases toxins, builds the navel centre.", "Same physiology as Kapālabhāti: hypocapnia, sympathetic activation, abdominal pumping.", "Weak.", "Before the track only (seated).", "As Kapālabhāti."],
    [21, "Long Deep Breathing (three-part yogic breath)", "Kundalini Yoga; Sivananda/Satyananda 'dirgha pranayama'", "Public", "Fill belly, then ribs, then collarbones; exhale top-down. 4–6 s each way; 1–3 min.", "4–6", "Any", "Fills the lungs fully, 'resets the magnetic field', calms.", "Full tidal volume with slow rate: HRV and vagal effect; teaches diaphragmatic breathing.", "Moderate.", "Threshold 3:00–4:00 (on the swell).", "—"],
    [22, "One-Minute Breath (20-20-20)", "Kundalini Yoga (Yogi Bhajan)", "Public", "Inhale 20 s, hold 20 s, exhale 20 s = one breath per minute; build from 5-5-5 and 10-10-10 over weeks. 3–11 min.", "1", "Seated", "'Optimises cooperation between the hemispheres', intuition, calmness.", "Extreme slow breathing with retention: strong CO2 rise during the hold, then washout; big HRV swings; trained practitioners only.", "Weak.", "Advanced option in Descent (instead of kriya pranayama).", "Not for beginners, pregnancy, heart or lung disease."],
    [23, "Sitali Pranayam (Kundalini form)", "Kundalini Yoga", "Public", "As Śītalī (#8), often 26 breaths morning and evening.", "5–6", "Any", "Cooling, 'gives power', lowers fever and anger.", "As #8.", "Weak.", "Return.", "—"],
    [24, "Segmented / sniff breathing (4-part in, 1 out etc.)", "Kundalini Yoga", "Public", "Inhale in 4 equal sniffs, exhale in one stroke (or 4:4, 8:8).", "6–10", "Seated", "Specific effects claimed per ratio (healing, energy, calm).", "Fractionated inhales act as a rhythmic attention task; no distinctive physiology.", "None.", "Not used.", "—"],
    # ---- Bihar / Satyananda
    [25, "Yoga Nidra breath counting (stage 4: 27 → 1)", "Bihar School of Yoga (Swami Satyananda, 1976; from tantric nyāsa)", "Public (book 'Yoga Nidra')", "Lying, count each breath backwards from 27 to 1 (one number per full breath, or per inhale and exhale), restarting if you lose the count.", "natural", "Lying", "Conscious entry into the hypnagogic state while remaining aware; the sankalpa is then 'planted'.", "A low-load cognitive task that keeps a thread of wakeful awareness at sleep onset; losing count = you drifted into theta/sleep.", "Moderate (Yoga Nidra trials on sleep, anxiety; 2023 EEG/fMRI studies).", "No-Time 31:00–37:00 (the thread of awareness).", "—"],
    [26, "Psychic breathing / ajapa with the spinal passage", "Bihar School (Satyananda's 'Ajapa Japa' series; 'Kriya Yoga' of the Bihar tradition, 76 kriyas)", "Public in Satyananda's books; the Bihar kriyas are taught in courses",
     "Ujjāyī + khecarī; visualise the breath rising from the navel to the throat (or mūlādhāra to ajna) on the inhale with 'So', descending on the exhale with 'Ham'; variants reverse the direction (Ham-So) or use a spiral.", "4–6", "Any", "Awakens the psychic passage (suṣumnā); prepares for kundalini kriyas.", "As kriya pranayama (#17) but shorter counts.", "Traditional.", "Descent.", "—"],
    # ---- Himalayan tradition (Swami Rama)
    [27, "2:1 breathing (exhale twice the inhale)", "Himalayan Tradition (Swami Rama, Himalayan Institute); also classical", "Public", "Inhale 4 s, exhale 8 s (or 5/10), no pauses, diaphragmatic, through the nose. 5–20 min.", "4–5", "Any", "Calms the mind; the long exhalation 'empties the body of carbon dioxide and the mind of worry'.", "Exhale-weighted breathing: heart rate falls on each exhale (RSA); overall parasympathetic shift; reduces anxiety in small RCTs.", "Moderate.", "Descent (combined with Ujjāyī and So'ham).", "—"],
    [28, "Diaphragmatic ('crocodile', makarāsana) breathing", "Himalayan Tradition", "Public", "Lie prone on the forearms (crocodile) or supine with a hand on the belly; breathe so only the belly moves; 5 min.", "6–10", "Prone/supine", "Establishes the diaphragm as the primary breathing muscle; the foundation of all pranayama.", "Diaphragmatic pattern lowers respiratory rate, increases tidal volume and vagal tone; reduces upper-chest accessory muscle tension.", "Moderate.", "Before the track; and whenever the breath goes to the chest.", "—"],
    [29, "Agni Sāra (fire essence)", "Himalayan Tradition; Hatha", "Public", "Standing bent forward or seated: exhale fully, hold out, pump the belly in and out 10–30 times, inhale. 3–5 rounds.", "—", "Standing/seated", "Kindles digestive fire; energises.", "Abdominal pumping during exhale-hold (bahya kumbhaka): visceral massage, sympathetic activation.", "Weak.", "Before the track (morning).", "Not after meals; not in pregnancy."],
    # ---- Proprietary modern Indian systems
    [30, "Shambhavi Mahamudra Kriya (21-minute practice)", "Isha Foundation (Sadhguru) — 'Inner Engineering'", "Proprietary / initiatory: taught only in the Inner Engineering programme; the sequence is partly described in published studies",
     "Published descriptions: a Sukha Kriya (alternate-nostril) section, 21 slow 'Aum' chants on the breath, a specific rapid-breathing cycle, retention with Shambhavi mudrā (eyes turned up to the brow), then sitting in stillness; 21 min, ideally twice daily. The generic Shambhavi mudrā itself is classical (HYP 4.35–37).", "varies", "Seated",
     "Opens the 'third eye' (ajna), 'activates the pineal', stabilises energy, 'becomes a receptive instrument for grace'.", "A composite of alternate-nostril breathing, chanting, brief hyperventilation and gaze-fixation: combined HRV, CO2 and attentional effects; published studies report better sleep and HRV in practitioners.",
     "Weak–moderate (Peterson et al. 2017 Int J Yoga; Braboszcz 2013 EEG — practitioner samples, not RCTs).", "The generic Shambhavi mudrā is used in Ajna (21–29 min). The full kriya: do it as taught, before the track.", "Follow the organisation's own instructions if initiated."],
    [31, "Sudarshan Kriya Yoga (SKY)", "Art of Living (Sri Sri Ravi Shankar, 1982)", "Proprietary: the full kriya is taught in courses and practised to a recorded cue tape; the components are public",
     "Course form: three-stage Ujjāyī (slow, 2–4/min, with arm positions), Bhastrikā (~30/min, 3 rounds), 'Om' ×3, then the Sudarshan Kriya proper: cyclical breathing in slow, medium and fast rhythms ('So-Ham') in repeated rounds for ~20–30 min, then rest.", "2–4, then 30, then 20–40 cycling", "Seated",
     "Removes stress, 'purifies the system', aligns body, breath and mind with the rhythms of nature.", "Deliberate alternation of hypoventilation and hyperventilation: swings in CO2 and autonomic tone that produce intense bodily sensations and emotional release; followed by deep rest (parasympathetic rebound).",
     "Moderate (RCTs: PTSD in veterans Seppälä 2014; depression trials; reviews by Brown & Gerbarg).", "Not inside the track. As taught, on a different day or well before.", "Hyperventilation cautions; organisation screens for conditions."],
    # ---- Tibetan / Bön
    [32, "Tummo / vase breathing (g-tummo, bum pa can)", "Vajrayana (Six Yogas of Nāropa); Bön; Kagyu/Gelug; expert nuns in Kozhevnikov's study", "Initiatory in Vajrayana (requires empowerment); the breathing mechanics are published; Wim Hof's method is a Western derivative",
     "Seated: inhale 5 s drawing the breath to the navel, swallow and lock (perineum lifted, abdomen gently pressed to form a 'vase'), hold 10–30 s visualising a flame at the navel rising up the central channel, exhale slowly 8 s. 5–7 rounds. Forceful versions add a vigorous 'blowing' phase.", "2–3", "Seated only",
     "Generates inner heat (tummo), burns the 'drops', melts bliss down the central channel; the preliminary for clear light and illusory body.", "Breath-hold with isometric abdominal contraction (Valsalva-like) raises intrathoracic pressure and, with the visualisation, measured core temperature by up to ~1 °C (axillary rises; EEG alpha/beta increases) — Kozhevnikov 2013.",
     "Moderate (PLoS ONE 2013; small samples).", "Before the track (seated, ≤3 min).", "Not with hypertension, heart disease, pregnancy; never in water."],
    [33, "Nine breaths of purification (Lung ro sel)", "Bön (Tenzin Wangyal Rinpoche); Nyingma/Dzogchen preliminaries", "Public in Bön teachings", "Alternate-nostril: 3 breaths out the right (release anger/white channel), 3 out the left (attachment/red channel), 3 out both (ignorance/central channel), with visualised coloured channels.", "4–6", "Seated", "Clears the three poisons from the three channels before practice.", "Alternate-nostril slow breathing with imagery: as #1.", "Traditional.", "Before the track.", "—"],
    [34, "Tsa lung (channel-wind) exercises", "Bön Dzogchen (Zhang Zhung Nyan Gyud); Tibetan yantra yoga", "Taught in courses; public books", "Five exercises: inhale, hold, move the body in a specific way while holding, exhale forcefully. Each clears one of five winds (upward, life-force, fire-like, pervasive, downward-clearing).", "—", "Seated", "Opens the chakras and channels so the mind can rest in its nature.", "Retention with movement; mild Valsalva.", "Traditional.", "Before the track.", "Short holds."],
    # ---- Taoist
    [35, "Embryonic breathing (tai xi, 胎息)", "Taoist inner alchemy (neidan; Baopuzi; 'Secret of the Golden Flower')", "Public in classical texts", "Lying or seated: breathing becomes so slow and fine 'a feather at the nostrils would not move'; attention in the lower dantian; the breath seems to stop, as in the womb.", "1–4", "Any", "Returns to the pre-natal breath; conserves jing; the body becomes 'a cauldron'.", "Voluntary hypoventilation: CO2 rises slightly, drowsiness/warmth; the same edge as kevala kumbhaka reached from the Taoist side.", "Traditional.", "No-Time (passively).", "Do not force."],
    [36, "Reverse (Taoist) breathing", "Taoist qigong; martial arts; neidan", "Public", "Abdomen draws IN on the inhale and expands on the exhale, perineum lifts on the inhale; slow, nasal.", "4–8", "Any", "Builds pressure in the dantian; 'compresses the qi'; used for the microcosmic orbit and for power in martial arts.", "Increases intra-abdominal pressure cycles; strong diaphragmatic/pelvic-floor coordination; no unique autonomic effect shown.", "Weak.", "Optional during the orbit (#37).", "—"],
    [37, "Microcosmic orbit (xiao zhou tian) with the breath", "Taoist neidan ('Secret of the Golden Flower', Wilhelm 1929; Mantak Chia's Universal Tao popularised it)", "Classical texts public; Chia's system taught in courses",
     "Inhale: attention rises from the perineum up the spine (Governing vessel) to the crown; exhale: descends down the front midline (Conception vessel) to the navel and perineum. One orbit per breath, 9–36 orbits. 'Turn the light around' (attention/light gathered between the eyes, then sunk to the Ni Wan at the head's centre).", "4–8", "Seated or lying",
     "Circulates qi; unites fire and water; the 'golden flower' opens; the heavenly eye (tian mu) opens; immortality of the spirit.", "A trained attentional circuit synchronised with breathing (the same family as kriya pranayama); Ni Wan/pineal equation is a 20th-century gloss.", "Traditional.", "Descent (alternative to kriya pranayama).", "—"],
    [38, "Six Healing Sounds (Liu Zi Jue)", "Taoist (Tao Hongjing, 5th–6th c.); standardised by the Chinese Health Qigong Association 2003", "Public", "One slow inhale, six whispered exhales: XU (liver), HE (heart), HU (spleen), SI (lungs), CHUI (kidneys), XI (triple burner); 6 breaths each, exhale 6–8 s; ~8 min.", "5–6", "Standing/seated", "Expels turbid qi from each organ; harmonises the five phases.", "Prolonged whispered exhales (vagal); EEG study (n=20) found frontal theta and alpha rising over 15–30 min of practice.", "Weak–moderate (Henz & Schöllhorn 2018).", "Before or after the track.", "—"],
    # ---- Buddhist
    [39, "Ānāpānasati (16 steps of mindfulness of breathing)", "Theravāda (Ānāpānasati Sutta, MN 118); Zen susokukan (counting) is the first tetrad", "Public (canonical)", "Four tetrads: body (long/short breath, whole body, calming), feelings (rapture, pleasure, mental formation, calming it), mind (experiencing, gladdening, concentrating, liberating), dhammas (impermanence, fading, cessation, relinquishment). Breath is never controlled.", "natural", "Seated/lying", "Fulfils the four foundations of mindfulness and the seven factors of awakening; leads to jhāna and liberation.", "Open-monitoring/focused-attention training: the most-studied meditation family (attention networks, DMN down-regulation, respiratory slowing).", "Strong for attention/anxiety outcomes (meta-analyses of mindfulness).", "The spine of the whole track: body (Threshold–Descent), feelings (Drum Gate–Ajna), mind (No-Time), dhammas (Crown–Return).", "—"],
    [40, "Susokukan (breath counting) / Zuisokukan (breath following)", "Zen (Rinzai and Sōtō); Hakuin's 'soft-butter' method is the lying-down relaxation cousin", "Public", "Count exhalations 1–10, start again; later just follow the breath; later shikantaza (just sitting).", "natural", "Seated", "Settles the mind before kōan or shikantaza.", "As Yoga Nidra counting (#25).", "Moderate.", "Threshold.", "—"],
    # ---- Sufi / Christian / Jewish
    [41, "Dhikr of the breath (Allāh-Hū; Lā ilāha illallāh on the breath; hosh dar dam)", "Sufi orders: Naqshbandī (awareness in every breath), Qādirī/Rifāʿī hadra (loud, accelerating), Chishtī", "Public in outline; the silent methods are given by a shaykh",
     "Silent: inhale 'Al-lāh' 4 s, exhale 'Hū' 6–8 s; or the negation-affirmation: 'Lā ilāha' drawn from the navel up to the brain on a held breath, 'illallāh' struck into the heart. Loud hadra: 'Hū' on every exhale, 60 → 120/min, bending forward on the exhale.", "5–6 (silent); 60–120 (hadra)", "Seated; standing for hadra",
     "Remembrance of God with every breath; the heart 'polished'; fanā (annihilation of self) and ecstasy (wajd).", "Silent form = coherent breathing with a two-syllable anchor; the loud accelerating form = rhythmic hyperventilation and vestibular movement, the classic trance-induction pair.", "Traditional; coherent-breathing physiology strong.", "Crown (the 'Hū' exhale alternative to A-OM-HUM).", "Hadra form not for this lying-down track."],
    [42, "Hesychast Jesus Prayer on the breath", "Eastern Orthodox monasticism (Mount Athos; Philokalia; Kallistos & Ignatios Xanthopoulos, 14th c.)", "Public (Philokalia; 'The Way of a Pilgrim')", "'Lord Jesus Christ, Son of God' on the inhale (4–5 s), 'have mercy on me' on the exhale (5–6 s), chin lowered, attention 'in the heart'; thousands of repetitions a day with a prayer rope.", "5–6", "Seated", "Ceaseless prayer; the mind descends into the heart; vision of the uncreated light (Palamas).", "Coherent (0.1 Hz) breathing with a verbal anchor — the Christian form of the same HRV-resonance practice.", "Strong for the breathing rate; traditional for the rest.", "Descent (alternative anchor).", "—"],
    [43, "Abulafia's letter-breath (tzeruf) with head movements", "Ecstatic Kabbalah (Abraham Abulafia, 13th c., 'Ḥayyei ha-ʿOlam ha-Ba')", "Public in scholarship (Idel, Scholem); practised rarely", "One Hebrew letter+vowel per full breath: inhale, pronounce on the exhale, head moves with the vowel (o: up; a: left→right; e: right→left; i: down; u: forward); permutations of the 72-letter Name; at night, in darkness.", "5–7", "Seated", "Prophecy (nevuʾah); 'untying the knots' of the soul; signs: trembling, warmth, inner light, a voice.", "Slow phonated exhales plus rhythmic head movement (vestibular) and a demanding permutation task: a strong absorption protocol.", "Traditional.", "Not used in the track (listed as the Jewish breath-sound technology).", "—"],
    # ---- Kashmir Shaivism / Siddha
    [44, "Vijñāna Bhairava breath dhāraṇās (the pauses; the turning points; 'neither in nor out')", "Kashmir Shaivism / Trika (Vijñāna Bhairava Tantra, vv. 24–31, c. 8th c.)", "Public (translations: Jaideva Singh; Lakshmanjoo)",
     "v.24: attend to the breath rising and falling and to the two places where it turns. v.25: rest in the pause after the inhale and the pause after the exhale. v.26: when the breath is held neither in nor out, the Self shines. v.27: retain at the end of the exhale (bahya kumbhaka) and the state of peace arises. No counts: pure attention.", "natural", "Any",
     "The gap between breaths is the gap between thoughts: Bhairava (absolute consciousness) is found there.", "Attention to respiratory pauses lengthens them (sleep-onset physiology) and interrupts the inner monologue; the oldest written description of what the No-Time stage is for.", "Traditional.", "No-Time 31:00–37:00.", "—"],
