PHYS_HDR = ["Phenomenon", "What it is — the physics", "What it does in the body — the physiology", "Evidence", "How this track uses it", "Does quantum mechanics enter? (honest answer)"]
PHYS = [
    ["Acoustic (monaural) beat", "Superposition: cos(2πf₁t) + cos(2πf₂t) = 2·cos(2π·(f₁−f₂)/2·t)·cos(2π·(f₁+f₂)/2·t). Two tones a few Hz apart in the same ear produce a real pressure wave whose loudness pulses at |f₁−f₂|. This is classical wave interference, the same mathematics as water waves or radio sidebands.",
     "The cochlea and brainstem receive an amplitude-modulated signal; neurons that phase-lock to the envelope produce the auditory steady-state response (ASSR) at the beat rate — strongest near 40 Hz.", "Strong (Schwarz & Taylor 2005; Picton 2003).", "Isochronic theta pulse (204 Hz carrier), 40 Hz gamma cues (544 Hz), bowl doublets split at the beat rate, Chavín conch cluster. These survive speakers and spatialisers.", "No. Sound in air and tissue is classical at these scales (wavelengths of metres, energies of ~10⁻¹⁰ J); nothing here involves superposition of quantum states."],
    ["Binaural beat", "No physical beat exists in the air: each ear gets one pure tone (e.g. 406.2 Hz and 410.4 Hz). The interaural phase difference rotates through 360° at 4.25 times per second; neurons in the superior olivary complex that compare arrival time between the ears fire in step with that rotation.",
     "A weak but real steady-state response at the beat frequency; small cross-frequency effects (binaural theta driving 40 Hz activity in one study); the subjective 'wobble'. Whole-brain 'entrainment' is contested (5 of 14 EEG studies support it).", "Strong for the percept; mixed for entrainment (Oster 1973; Orozco Perez 2020; Ingendoh 2023).", "Three carriers 408.3 / 272.2 / 136.1 Hz, beat ladder 10 → 2.13 → 14.1 Hz.", "No. Neural phase comparison is electrochemical; the ~1 ms timing precision of the medial superior olive is remarkable but classical."],
    ["Entrainment (coupled oscillators)", "Christiaan Huygens, 1665: two pendulum clocks on one beam fall into step — the origin of the word. A driven oscillator locks to a periodic force when the drive is strong enough relative to the frequency mismatch (the 'Arnold tongue'). Lock-in is a classical nonlinear-dynamics phenomenon.",
     "Rhythmic sensory input can phase-lock ongoing neural oscillations near the drive frequency (steady-state responses; photic driving; drum 'auditory driving'); the brain's own rhythms (alpha ~10 Hz etc.) are the oscillators. Cardio-respiratory synchronisation (heartbeats locking to breath) is a measured example in humans (Schäfer et al., Nature 1998).", "Strong for steady-state responses; moderate for drumming; weak for lasting state change.", "Every pulsed layer; the slow beat ramps (≤1.2 Hz/min) keep the drive inside the lock-in region as the target frequency moves.", "No — but this is the real meaning of 'mastery of frequency and vibration': you own several oscillators (heart, breath, brain rhythms, circadian clock) and this practice is about coupling them deliberately."],
    ["Resonance", "A system with a natural frequency stores energy from a drive at that frequency; amplitude peaks at f₀ with sharpness Q. Singing bowls, temple bells, stone chambers and the Earth–ionosphere cavity are all resonators.", "The ear canal resonates near 3 kHz; the chest near 100–150 Hz (why humming at 136 Hz is felt in the sternum); the skull/sinuses at 100–300 Hz (the 'head buzz').", "Strong (acoustics).", "Bowls from the measured thin-shell mode law; bonshō from measured modes; the 108.9 Hz chamber drone with long reverb; 136/272 Hz hum.", "No. Resonance is classical; 'resonating with the universe' is a metaphor built on this real phenomenon."],
    ["Schumann resonance", "Standing electromagnetic waves in the cavity between the Earth's surface and the ionosphere: f_n ≈ 7.5·√(n(n+1)) Hz → 7.83, 14.1, 20.3, 26… Hz, excited by ~50 lightning strikes per second worldwide. Discovered theoretically by W. O. Schumann 1952, measured 1960.",
     "The field is ~1 picotesla — about a million times weaker than the Earth's static field and far below anything neurons respond to. Claimed EEG–Schumann coupling rests on small unreplicated studies (Pobachenko 2006; Saroka & Persinger).", "The resonance: strong. Biological effect: weak/none.", "7.83 Hz as the Descent and Crown plateau rate and 14.1 Hz as the SMR return rate — symbolic coherence, labelled as such.", "No; it is classical electromagnetism (Maxwell's equations in a spherical cavity)."],
    ["HRV resonance / coherent breathing (~0.1 Hz)", "The baroreflex loop (blood pressure → baroreceptors → heart rate → blood pressure) has a delay of about 5 s, giving it a resonant frequency near 0.1 Hz. Breathing at that rate drives the loop at resonance and the oscillation amplitude of heart rate becomes maximal.", "High-amplitude respiratory sinus arrhythmia, vagal activation, lower BP; improves anxiety, depression, asthma, hypertension markers across many RCTs; the 'coherence' state of HeartMath is this.", "Strong (Lehrer & Gevirtz 2014).", "The breath pacer swell at 6/min; the protocol's Descent and Return breathing.", "No. It is control-systems physics (a delayed negative-feedback loop) — but it is the most reliable 'vibration-control' effect in the entire programme."],
    ["CO₂, pH and breath retention", "Breathing rate sets arterial CO₂. Hyperventilation lowers it (respiratory alkalosis): cerebral vessels constrict, Ca²⁺ ionisation falls → tingling, cramps, light-headedness, altered consciousness. Hypoventilation/retention raises CO₂: vasodilation, warmth, drowsiness, urge to breathe.", "Explains the 'energy' sensations of Kapalabhati/Bhastrika/Wim Hof/SKY, the calm after retention, the warmth of slow kriya breathing, and the heaviness at the floor of the session.", "Strong (textbook respiratory physiology).", "Why the fast techniques are kept seated and before the track, and the slow ones inside it.", "No."],
    ["Nitric oxide and humming", "NO is produced in the paranasal sinuses; oscillating airflow from humming increases sinus gas exchange by ~15× relative to quiet exhalation.", "NO dilates airways and blood vessels, is antimicrobial, and improves pulmonary oxygen uptake; the mechanism behind the 'bee breath' benefits.", "Moderate (Weitzberg & Lundberg 2002, n=10; replications small).", "Resonant tuning hum; vowel toning; A-OM-HUM.", "No."],
    ["Vagal afferents and chanting", "The auricular branch of the vagus nerve innervates the ear canal/concha; vibration and pressure there (as in OM chanting, humming) can stimulate it, resembling transcutaneous VNS.", "fMRI during OM chanting (n=12) showed deactivation of orbitofrontal cortex, amygdala, hippocampus and thalamus — the signature of limbic quieting.", "Weak–moderate (pilot).", "Self-produced sound in the protocol (not just listening).", "No."],
    ["Hypnagogia (the border of sleep)", "Not physics but the key physiological state: as alpha drops out, theta ripples appear, then vertex sharp waves and sleep spindles (Hori's 9 EEG stages). Dream-like imagery, body-image distortions, floating, 'vibrations', sounds and voices arise here.", "This is the state in which Monroe's 'vibrational state', Neville's SATS, Yoga Nidra's sankalpa, shamanic journeying and lucid-dream induction all operate.", "Strong description; effects of practices moderate.", "The whole arc: the beat ladder parks you at this edge (Descent → No-Time) and the alpha stack + counting keep you from falling through.", "No."],
    ["Gamma (25–45 Hz)", "Fast cortical oscillations associated with binding, attention and, in long-term meditators, sustained high-amplitude synchrony (Lutz 2004). The 40 Hz ASSR is the most robust evoked oscillation.", "40 Hz sensory stimulation entrains auditory cortex and hippocampus in humans (Chan 2022); 40 Hz tACS during REM raised dream lucidity (Voss 2014); 40 Hz binaural/monaural beats improved working memory in one trial.", "Moderate.", "Four 90-second 40 Hz isochronic cues in No-Time and one in Crown.", "No; 40 Hz is an electrophysiological rhythm. Penrose–Hameroff proposed 40 Hz gamma as the readout of microtubule quantum events (Orch-OR) — a hypothesis without experimental confirmation."],
    ["Out-of-body and 'travel' experiences", "Vestibular–multisensory conflict at the temporo-parietal junction produces the sense of being outside the body; sleep paralysis adds felt presence and buzzing; the hypnagogic state supplies imagery.", "Electrical stimulation of the TPJ reproducibly evokes OBEs (Blanke 2002); sleep paralysis has 7.6 % lifetime prevalence; Monroe's descriptions match these states point for point.", "Strong (neuroscience of OBE); the experiences are real experiences, not verified travel.", "The No-Time stage is engineered to reach this edge; the guide tells you what the sensations are.", "No. The CIA 'Gateway' memo's physics (Bohm's implicate order, Pribram's holographic brain, 'click-out' below Planck length) is speculative metaphysics layered on these states, not an experimental result."],
    ["Time perception and 'no time'", "Subjective time depends on dopaminergic pacemaker-accumulator circuits and on interoceptive event density; fewer bodily and mental events → time compresses or vanishes.", "Deep absorption, hypnagogia and slow breathing reduce event density → 'timelessness'. Reliably reported; entirely internal.", "Moderate.", "The 2.13 Hz floor with stilled breath.", "No. Relativity (time dilation) and quantum mechanics describe physical clocks, not felt duration; nothing in a lying body approaches relativistic speeds or gravitational gradients."],
    ["The pineal gland and light", "Photons absorbed by melanopsin in retinal ganglion cells (a quantum event: one photon → one isomerisation) signal the suprachiasmatic nucleus, which gates pineal melatonin via sympathetic fibres from the superior cervical ganglion. Sound has no pathway to the pineal.", "Melatonin rhythm; blue light suppresses it ~2× longer than green at equal brightness; calcification is age-related and unaffected by any known intervention; pineal DMT is measured in rats, not humans, at non-psychoactive levels.", "Strong.", "Darkness, eye mask, no screens before evening sessions — the only honest 'pineal' levers.", "Yes, here: photoreception is quantum (single-photon detection, isomerisation of 11-cis-retinal/retinaldehyde). It is why darkness matters and why sound cannot do this job."],
    ["Quantum biology — what is actually established", "Quantum effects that are measured in living systems: photosynthetic energy transfer coherence (femtoseconds), enzyme proton/electron tunnelling, the radical-pair mechanism in cryptochrome for bird magnetoreception (a genuine spin-chemistry effect), photoreception. All occur in warm, wet tissue but on femto- to microsecond timescales inside protected molecules.",
     "None of these mechanisms is known to be influenced by breathing, sound or intention; none scales up to neuron-level computation.", "Strong for photosynthesis/tunnelling; moderate for magnetoreception.", "Perspective only.", "Yes, but confined to molecular events. The sentence 'everything is vibration/frequency' is true of quantum fields and of classical waves alike; it does not license control of outcomes by intention."],
    ["Quantum consciousness (Orch-OR) and decoherence", "Penrose & Hameroff: consciousness arises from orchestrated objective reduction of quantum states in neuronal microtubules. Tegmark (2000) calculated decoherence times of 10⁻¹³–10⁻²⁰ s in the brain, far shorter than neural timescales (10⁻³ s); Hameroff disputes the assumptions. 2022 work on tryptophan superradiance in microtubules shows quantum-optical effects in the molecules themselves.",
     "No experiment shows that quantum coherence in microtubules affects neural firing, let alone consciousness.", "None (hypothesis).", "Not used. Mentioned because it is the one serious physics proposal behind 'quantum mind' language.", "Open question in principle; no empirical support in practice; and even if true it would not imply that intention controls external matter."],
    ["The 'observer effect' and intention", "In quantum mechanics 'measurement' means any irreversible interaction with the environment; it does not require a conscious observer. The von Neumann–Wigner 'consciousness causes collapse' interpretation exists but is a minority view with no experimental support; decoherence theory explains measurement without it.", "Experiments claiming mind-matter interaction (PEAR random-number studies; Radin's double-slit 'intention' experiments) have not replicated under independent conditions (the 2000 multi-lab PEAR replication was null).", "None.", "The protocol's 'manifestation' window is a hypnagogic imagination practice (SATS/sankalpa), which affects you — your attention, mood and choices — not external probability.", "No. This is the single most common misuse of quantum language in the field."],
    ["Energy, frequency, vibration (the Tesla line)", "'If you want to find the secrets of the universe, think in terms of energy, frequency and vibration' appears in none of Tesla's writings; it is a late-20th-century attribution. The physics content is nonetheless true in a precise sense: every field has modes, every mode has a frequency, and E = hf for quanta.", "What you can actually control: breath rate (0.05–2 Hz), heart-rate variability (0.1 Hz resonance), vocal fundamental (100–300 Hz), EEG rhythm bias (via attention and via entrainment), circadian phase (via light). That is the 'frequency mastery' available to a human body, and it is considerable.", "—", "Every layer of the track is a handle on one of those oscillators.", "E = hf is quantum; the oscillators you control are classical."],
    ["Cymatics and 'water memory'", "Chladni/cymatic patterns are standing waves on plates or fluid surfaces — real, beautiful, classical. Masaru Emoto's water-crystal photographs were not produced under controlled conditions; the one quasi-controlled attempt (Radin & Emoto 2008) is widely criticised.", "No biological mechanism.", "None for healing claims.", "Not used.", "No."],
    ["Loudness, dB and hearing", "Sound pressure level in dB SPL is logarithmic: +10 dB ≈ twice as loud; WHO weekly allowance 80 dBA for 40 h. The track is mastered at −16 LUFS integrated with −3.5 dBTP peaks so that a comfortable headphone setting lands near the 65–70 dB SPL used in the EEG studies.", "Hair-cell damage is cumulative and irreversible; low carriers sound quieter than they measure.", "Strong.", "Mastering targets; the guide's 'quieter than conversation' instruction.", "No."],
]

